---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How to Print Large-Print PDFs | Caring for Aging Parents"
  description="How to print our large-print caregiving sheets on US Letter or A4 paper at 100% scale in black and white, plus the 30-second readability test."
  canonicalPath="/how-to-print/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: "How to Print", href: "/how-to-print/" }]}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="prose-measure space-y-10">
      <header>
        <h1 class="font-serif text-teal">
          How to print our sheets so they stay large and easy to read
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-ink">
          Every printable PDF we make is designed in large print, so it is easy to read and easy to fill in by hand without cramping your fingers. Follow these simple printer settings so your home printer does not accidentally shrink the page.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border-2 border-warm-border bg-cream">
        <img
          src="/images/guides/guide-print.webp"
          width="1200"
          height="630"
          alt="Flat illustration of a home printer and hands holding up a large-print checklist sheet."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="h-auto w-full object-cover"
        />
      </div>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-teal">
          1. US Letter vs. A4 paper
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
          All of our PDF files are laid out for standard <strong>US Letter paper (8.5 by 11 inches)</strong>, which is the everyday copy paper sold across the United States and Canada.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
          If you live in a country that uses <strong>A4 paper (210 by 297 mm)</strong>, A4 is slightly narrower and slightly taller than US Letter. You can still print every sheet cleanly: on US Letter paper choose <strong>100% / Actual Size</strong>, and on A4 paper choose <strong>Fit to Printable Area</strong> so the left and right borders stay intact.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-teal">
          2. Set scale to 100% (Actual Size)
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
          Many laptops and phone print menus automatically select "Shrink to Fit" or "Fit to Page." Even when you already have US Letter paper loaded, that setting can shrink the text and writing boxes noticeably.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
          In your print dialog box, look for <strong>Scale</strong> or <strong>Page Sizing</strong> and choose <strong>Actual Size</strong> or type <strong>100%</strong>. Our sheets already have wide, safe outer margins built in, including extra space on the left edge if you want to three-hole punch them for a binder.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-teal">
          3. Choose Black &amp; White (Grayscale) mode
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
          Our sheets are built specifically for black-and-white printing. Selecting <strong>Black &amp; White</strong> or <strong>Grayscale</strong> in your printer window forces the printer to use crisp black ink rather than mixing color cartridges. Turn off "Draft" or "Toner Save" mode so lines and headings come out solid and dark.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-teal">
          4. Pick the right paper weight
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-ink">
          <li>
            <strong>Standard 20 lb copy paper:</strong> Good for single-sided weekly handover sheets kept on a clipboard or slipped inside clear matte binder sleeves.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Heavier 24 lb or 28 lb matte paper:</strong> Best for the Who-to-Call Sheet on the refrigerator door or pages you handle every day. Thicker paper stays flat, resists tearing, and prevents felt-tip pen ink from showing through.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Avoid glossy paper:</strong> Always use plain matte paper so kitchen lamps and ceiling lights do not reflect glare into your parent's eyes.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="rounded-xl border-2 border-teal bg-cream p-6 sm:p-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-teal">
          5. The 30-second test
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
          Before you print a full pack, print <strong>one page</strong> and do this quick check:
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 font-bold text-ink">
          Print it, hold it at arm's length under the normal light in the room where it will live, and ask: can you read it comfortably?
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink">
          Then pick up a dark rollerball or felt-tip pen and write one name and phone number in a box. If you can read the headings easily at arm's length and your handwriting fits without crowding, your printer setup is ready.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="font-serif text-teal">
          Quick printing checklist
        </h2>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-ink">
          <li>Paper loaded: plain white matte US Letter paper (20 lb to 28 lb)</li>
          <li>Orientation: Portrait (vertical)</li>
          <li>Scale: 100% or Actual Size (or Fit to Printable Area if using A4)</li>
          <li>Color mode: Black &amp; White / Grayscale, Normal quality (not Draft)</li>
          <li>Single-sided printing selected so each sheet can lie flat on a clipboard</li>
          <li>Passed the 30-second arm's-length readability test in kitchen light</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
